## Spec Organization

Specs are organized into **platform specs** (shared) and **subsystem specs** (one per
module or driver). Every subsystem spec carries a **formal layer**: an abstract model in
Rust, invariants, contracts, properties, and trust assumptions. See
`docs/spec-strategy.md` for the methodology.

```
docs/
├── spec-strategy.md              # Methodology for all specs
├── spec/                         # Platform-level shared specs
│   ├── index.md                  # Start here - overview + links to subsystems
│   ├── hardware.md               # Board, SoC, cores, memory map, boot, bench
│   ├── stack.md                  # Toolchain, target, prover, build matrix
│   ├── verification.md           # Prover, assurance levels, TCB, ID scheme, design rules
│   ├── properties.md             # Property registry - every INV/PROP/TA, traceability
│   ├── build-order.md            # Platform build order with property gates
│   ├── model.rs                  # Shared model types (model::platform)
│   ├── templates/                # Templates for new subsystems
│   └── archive/                  # Historical specs
│
├── {{subsystem}}/spec/           # Subsystem specs
│   ├── index.md                  # Overview + Related Platform Specs table
│   ├── {{subsystem}}.md          # The spec: model, INVs, operations, PROPs, TAs, harness plan
│   └── model.rs                  # Abstract model (model::{{subsystem}})
│
└── {{another-subsystem}}/spec/
    └── ...

model/                            # The model crate - includes every model.rs by path
```

**When working on a subsystem:**
1. Read its spec (`docs/{{subsystem}}/spec/{{subsystem}}.md`) and its `model.rs`
2. Check the "Related Specs" table at the top
3. Check its rows in `docs/spec/properties.md`
4. Consult `verification.md` for design rules and harness conventions

**Spec status:** ✅ Complete / 🔲 Pending / 🔲 Partial / 🔲 Draft
**Verification status (per ID):** 🔲 Specified / 🔲 Harnessed / ✅ Verified (N) / ✅ Tested / ✅ Trusted

